;;; A rule's `:match` splits a name that starts with `and` —
;;; regression `kw-and-in-match` (`corpus/regressions.toml`): a reserved word is
;;; a keyword only as a whole token (Q-M1e2.17).
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ──────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two facts, `(p a)` and `(andz b)`, and one rule, `r`, concluding `(hit
;;; ?x)` from the premise `(andz ?x)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A keyword is a whole word: `01-grammar.md` § 1, fact 1, excludes a reserved
;;; word from `SYMBOL` only where a word boundary follows it (`neq` is not a
;;; `SYMBOL`; `neq_test` is), so `andz` is a name like any other. `r` fires on
;;; `(andz b)`, so root holds `(hit b)`. The blind enumerator proposes `(andz
;;; a)`, `(hit a)` and `(p b)`, and R is six states, the answer of
;;; `kw-and-in-match-twin.ein`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ────────────
;;;
;;; At `35186bc0` the parser read a reserved word as a prefix of a name, and
;;; this program answered wrong. The parser read the premise `(andz ?x)` as the
;;; conjunction `(and z ?x)`, of the atom `z` and the variable `?x`, which bound
;;; nothing: every command that saturated stopped at `r`, exit 1, with *unbound
;;; var ?x in :assert — bindings: {}* (`ein saturate`: prefixed `KeyError:`).
;;; (`ein solve FILE` at `35186bc0`, release and debug; every mode and `ein
;;; oracle` the same.) A keyword is a whole word now, at the boundary
;;; `01-grammar.md` § 1 states (Q-M1e2.17), and the program answers its twin's R.
;;;

(relation p Node :why "p holds of {?1}") (relation andz Node :why "andz holds of {?1}") (relation hit Node :why "{?1} is a hit")
(p a) (andz b)
(rule r () :match (andz ?x) :assert (hit ?x) :why "andz holds of {?x}, so {?x} is a hit")
